<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Republic of Central Albania</h1><p class="st-meta">1913 CE – 1914 CE · capital Durrës</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Rival Albanian government set up by Essad Pasha Toptani in Durrës in 1913, a symptom of the struggle for power in the newly independent country.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/essad-pasha-toptani">Essad Pasha Toptani</a>, an Ottoman general from a leading landowning family of central <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/albania">Albania</a>, commanded the defence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shkoder">Shkodër</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-balkan-war">First Balkan War</a>, then surrendered it to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/montenegro">Montenegro</a> in April 1913 in exchange for safe passage. Opposing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-qemali">Ismail Qemali's</a> <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/provisional-government-of-albania">Provisional Government of Albania</a>, he set up his own government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Durrës</a> in October 1913.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/essad-pasha-toptani">Essad</a> ruled central <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/albania">Albania</a>, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Durrës</a> to around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirana">Tirana</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbasan">Elbasan</a>, through a senate of local notables and the support of Muslim landowners and his own armed retainers. He cultivated ties with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/serbia">Serbia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. The state was essentially his personal domain and was never recognised by the Great Powers.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/essad-pasha-toptani">Essad</a> accepted the authority of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-prince-of-albania">Wilhelm of Wied</a> in early 1914, becoming minister of the interior and war in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-albania">Principality of Albania</a>, but was expelled for intrigue in May 1914. He returned later in 1914 to rule central <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/albania">Albania</a> until 1916, and was assassinated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1920.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/republic-of-central-albania.jpg" alt="Republic of Central Albania" width="320" height="214"><figcaption>Image: Vectorized by Kj1595 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Essad_Pasha%27s_flag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1913 CE – 1914 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Durrës</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Essad Pasha Toptani</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Albanian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Albania</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Essad Pasha's Albania</dd></dl></aside></div>
